{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 10, :comments ["<b>You shall not plow with an ox and a donkey.</b> For the ox continually chews the cud and eats, while the donkey does not chew the cud; the result is that this one eats while that one goes hungry — and this is suffering of living creatures. Another interpretation: because the ox is king among animals, and upon its likeness is the Throne of Glory, while the donkey is a despised animal — the pairing is unfitting. Another interpretation: the compassion of HaShem is upon all His works, and the strength of the donkey is not like the strength of the ox." "<b>Together.</b> Bound on one side — to exclude if he bound them on another side. And our Sages said: the same applies to all two species, as we derive 'ox-ox' from the Sabbath, and a person is permitted with all of them. And it was placed here because it spoke of the matter of sowing above, therefore it juxtaposed sha'atnez to it."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :chizkuni, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:53:48.528723064Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}